  "_commit": {
    "id": "78f6eb74f1f86a4bd3fe76cf65bfb0405b346edc",
    "author": "Patrick Aboyoun <aboyoun.patrick@gene.com>",
    "committer": "Patrick Aboyoun <aboyoun.patrick@gene.com>",
    "message": "Clip setdiff results to the sequence lengths, as GRanges does (0.99.13)\n\nGRanges builds setdiff() on gaps(), so its result lies within\n[1, seqlength] of each sequence whose length is known. setdiff() on a\nDuckDBGRanges returned ranges that run past the end untrimmed: with a\nlength of 100, 85-145 came back as 85-145 where GRanges gives 85-100.\n\n- Rename the existing algorithm to .setdiff_unclipped() and have the\n  method clip its result with the new .clip_to_seqlengths(), which\n  clamps each range to [1, seqlength], drops a range that falls wholly\n  outside, and leaves a sequence of unknown length unclipped. This also\n  covers the early returns, such as an empty second set.\n- union() and intersect() do not trim in GRanges and already matched it\n  with ranges past the end, so they are unchanged.\n\nThe union() merge of 85-145 and 165-166 into 85-166 that I had noted\ndoes not reproduce: 120 random union cases with ranges past the sequence\nend and 80 reduce cases all match GRanges, and the reported pair stays\nseparate at lengths NA, 100, 150 and 300. It may have been fixed by the\nordering change in 0.99.12 or come from a faulty fixture. Add a test\nthat keeps it that way.\n\nTests compare setdiff() with a GRanges for ranges past the end of a\nsequence, an empty second set, an unknown length, and random ranges\nwith start positions below 1; they fail on the previous code (26\nfailures). Suite: 1424 expectations, 0 failures at 1 and 8 threads.\n",
    "time": 1795277640
  },
